//@version=5
indicator("Sav FX - Cycles & Devil Marks", shorttitle="Sav FX", overlay=false, max_boxes_count=500, max_lines_count=500, max_labels_count=500)

// ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
//  MULTI-LEVEL QUARTERLY CYCLES
//
//  Every unit of time is split into four sub units, so each level is a quarter
//  of the level above it:
//
//    row              cell          numbers
//    1 Quadrennial     1 year        which year of the four year cycle, 2023 .. 2026
//    2 Yearly          13 weeks      which quarter of the year, by the week's Tuesday
//    3 Quarter Split   ~16 bars      which quarter of the quarter, by trading days
//    4 Weekly          1 week        which week of the month
//    5 Daily           1 day         which day of the week, Mon 1 .. Thu 4
//    6 6 Hour          6 hours       which session: Asia 1, London 2, AM 3, PM 4
//    7 90 Minute       90 min        which 90 min of the session
//    8 22.5 Minute     22.5 min      which 22.5 min of the 90 min
//    9 5.625 Minute    5.625 min     which 5.625 min of the 22.5 min
//
//  Every cell is labelled with the chain of quarters that leads to it, from the
//  highest of three levels down to its own: a 22.5 min cell reading  1-3-4  is
//  Asia session -> 3rd 90 min -> 4th 22.5 min, and the 5.625 min cells inside it
//  read 3-4-1 .. 3-4-4.  "Levels Per Label" sets how many links are printed. A
//  level that is on the chart but carries no number, Friday and a dead week,
//  holds its place with a mark, so a row reads the same levels in the same order
//  whatever the day.
//
//  THE YEAR AND ITS QUARTERS, which is what the WEEKLY chart measures. The
//  year opens on the MONDAY OF THE WEEK HOLDING THE FIRST TUESDAY OF JANUARY,
//  and every week belongs to the year and the quarter of its own TUESDAY. The
//  year is four quarters of exactly THIRTEEN WEEKS, Q1 being January, except
//  that a 53 week year gives the extra week to Q4, which runs fourteen. Each
//  quarter opens on a Monday and closes on the Sunday before the next opens:
//    2024   1 January,  1 April,  1 July,  30 September (Q4 is 14 weeks)
//    2025   6 January,  7 April,  7 July,   6 October
//    2026   5 January,  6 April,  6 July,   5 October, closing 3 January 2027
//
//  THE QUARTER OF THE QUARTER, which is what the DAILY chart measures. The
//  quarter's TRADING DAYS are divided in four: a cell opens after a quarter, a
//  half and three quarters of them, rounded to the nearest whole day with a
//  half rounding up. Trading days are the weekdays less nine exchange
//  holidays - New Year's Day, MLK, Presidents, Memorial, Juneteenth,
//  Independence, Labor, Thanksgiving and Christmas, each moved off a weekend
//  except a Saturday New Year's Day, which stays put and so costs nothing.
//  GOOD FRIDAY IS A TRADING DAY: the futures trade it, and that is the one
//  place this calendar parts company with the equity one. 2026 reads:
//    Q1  63 trading days    5 January, 28 January, 20 February, 13 March
//    Q2  62                 6 April, 28 April, 19 May, 11 June
//    Q3  64                 6 July, 28 July, 19 August, 11 September
//    Q4  62                 5 October, 27 October, 17 November, 10 December
//
//  Each cell runs to the day before the next, and the fourth to the end of its
//  weekly quarter. A turn day belongs to the quarter it opens, never to the one
//  it closes: 6 April is the first day of Q2 2026, not the last of Q1.
//
//  Cell edges sit on real bars, never on a computed calendar time. A cell opens
//  half a bar before its own first candle, and it is closed where the next cell
//  opens, so the two share one coordinate and the rows meet exactly. The
//  calendar start of a cell is no good on its own, because it is not always a
//  trading day: 1 January 2024 is a Monday and a holiday, so Q1 2024 opens on
//  the 2nd. A computed edge there would float inside a data gap, where
//  TradingView places it by the average bar spacing and it drifts on to candles
//  that belong to the quarter before.
//
//  The slow rows read as names and the fast ones as numbers, so the day row says
//  Wed where the 22.5 min row says 1-3-4: on a high timeframe those cells are far
//  too narrow to hold a three link chain. "Names Instead Of Numbers" moves the
//  line between the two, and the numbers can be printed after the name as well.
//
//  A cell whose three link chain is one of the marked sequences takes its
//  quarter's strong colour, the same colour as the rest of the row only
//  stronger. The sequences are an input, and default to every chain built only
//  of 1s and 4s plus the same number three times. The mark goes off the chain,
//  so it shows on every timeframe and on a row that prints a name just as much
//  as on one that prints numbers.
//
//  Every row whose cells are a fixed length also draws the next blocks ahead of
//  the last bar, dashed, so the one running and the ones after it are read off
//  the same ladder. The year, the quarter and the quarter split vary in length
//  and are left out.
//
//  Per timeframe: the monthly and weekly charts show the four year cycle and its
//  quarters, the daily chart the current year, its quarters and their splits, and
//  the week row starts on 12H and below. Lower down a row drops off its own end
//  as a finer one comes in, so the pane never stacks more than four of them: the
//  week row ends below 30m, and the daily row holds on down to 5m, where the
//  chart reads day - session - 90 min - 22.5 min. The 5.625 min row only comes
//  in below 5m, and the daily row steps aside for it there. A cell a bar or two
//  wide has no room for a three link chain, so drop "Levels Per Label" on the
//  fastest charts.
//
//  Levels that carry no quarter number are drawn without colour and without a
//  number, and they contribute no link to the chain:
//    - Friday, the fifth trading day of the week
//    - a dead week, whose Tuesday and Thursday fall in different months
//    - Saturday and Sunday, on symbols that trade them
//
//  TIME BASE, shared by everything in this script
//
//  The trading day opens at 18:00 on the New York wall clock, and nothing else:
//  the rows, the cell edges and the dividers all read the same clock, so none
//  of them can sit an hour from another. It holds through both daylight saving
//  changeovers, and it does not care what zone the chart or the exchange is on,
//  so there is nothing to put right by hand when a country moves its clocks a
//  week before or after New York does. The shift is left in the settings for
//  moving the ladder on purpose, and belongs on 0.
//
//  DEVIL MARKS, also on the candles
//
//  A candle whose body runs to one end with no wick there leaves a short line at
//  that edge, born on close and removed once price trades back through it.
// ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════


// ───────────────────────────────── inputs ─────────────────────────────────────
g_tz = "Time Base"
shiftH = input.float(0, "Shift From New York (hours)", minval = -12, maxval = 12, step = 0.25, group = g_tz, tooltip = "Leave this on 0. The whole script runs on the New York wall clock, so the trading day opens at 18:00 and London at 00:00 New York on every day of the year, the two daylight saving changeovers included, and whatever clock your own country or the chart is on. This is only here in case you ever want the ladder deliberately moved: a quarter of an hour at a time, either way.")

// The model's own clock. Everything reads off it: the rows, the cell edges and
// the dividers alike, so none of them can ever sit an hour from another.
gridTZ  = "America/New_York"
shiftMs = int(shiftH * 3600000)

g_cyc = "Time Cycles"
showCyc4   = input.bool(true, "Quadrennial Cycles", group = g_cyc, tooltip = "One cell per year: which year of the four year cycle it is. 2023 opens the cycle and 2026 closes it, so 2023 is year 1 and 2026 is year 4.")
showQtYear = input.bool(true, "Yearly Cycles",      group = g_cyc, tooltip = "One cell per quarter: which quarter of the year it is. The year opens on the Monday of the week holding the first Tuesday of January, and is four quarters of exactly thirteen weeks, Q1 being January; a 53 week year gives the extra week to Q4. Every week belongs to the quarter of its own Tuesday. 2026 is 5 January, 6 April, 6 July and 5 October, closing on 3 January 2027.")
showSplit  = input.bool(true, "Quarter Split",      group = g_cyc, tooltip = "One cell per quarter of a quarter, on the daily chart only. The weekly quarter's trading days are divided in four: a cell opens after a quarter, a half and three quarters of them, rounded to the nearest whole day with a half rounding up. Trading days are the weekdays less nine exchange holidays; Good Friday is a trading day, because the futures trade it. 2026 reads: Q1 63 trading days, 5 January, 28 January, 20 February, 13 March; Q2 62, 6 April, 28 April, 19 May, 11 June; Q3 64, 6 July, 28 July, 19 August, 11 September; Q4 62, 5 October, 27 October, 17 November, 10 December.")
showWeekly = input.bool(true, "Weekly Cycles",      group = g_cyc, tooltip = "One cell per week: which week of the month it is. A week belongs to the month holding its Tuesday.")
showDaily  = input.bool(true, "Daily Cycles",       group = g_cyc, tooltip = "One cell per trading day: which day of the week it is, Mon 1 .. Thu 4. Friday carries no number.")
show6H     = input.bool(true, "6 Hour Cycles",      group = g_cyc, tooltip = "One cell per session: Asia 1, London 2, AM 3, PM 4.")
show90     = input.bool(true, "90 Minute",          group = g_cyc)
show225    = input.bool(true, "22.5 Minute",        group = g_cyc)
show5625   = input.bool(true, "5.625 Minute",       group = g_cyc)

g_lab = "Labels"
showLabels  = input.bool(true, "Show Labels", group = g_lab)
nameRows    = input.string("Down to the session", "Names Instead Of Numbers", options = ["Down to the session", "Down to the day", "Off"], group = g_lab, tooltip = "The slow rows read better as names than as numbers, and their cells are too narrow for a three link chain on a high timeframe. This picks how far down the names go: to the session row (2026, Q3, Week 3, Wed, Asia), only to the daily row, or off for numbers everywhere. Everything below the line prints numbers.")
appendNums  = input.bool(false, "Add Numbers After The Name", group = g_lab, tooltip = "Prints both, as Wed 3-3-3. Leave it off where the cell is too narrow to hold the two of them.")
labelDepth  = input.int(3, "Levels Per Label", minval = 1, maxval = 3, group = g_lab, tooltip = "How many levels of the hierarchy each numbered cell prints. 3 gives 3-4-2 (grandparent-parent-self), 2 gives 4-2, 1 gives 2.")
sepStr      = input.string("-", "Separator", options = ["-", ".", " "], group = g_lab)
chainOrder  = input.string("Highest level first", "Chain Order", options = ["Highest level first", "Own level first"], group = g_lab, tooltip = "Which end of the chain the cell's own number sits at. Highest level first reads 1-4-2 as week 1, Thursday, London, with the cell itself last; own level first turns the same cell into 2-4-1. Every row uses the one setting, so the chain always reads the same way down the whole ladder.")
gapMark     = input.string("·", "Mark For A Level With No Number", group = g_lab, tooltip = "What a level that carries no number prints inside a chain: Friday, and a dead week. It holds the link's place, so every row keeps reading the same levels in the same order on a Friday as on a Monday. Empty drops the link instead, and the chain then pulls the level above it in to stay three long.")
textSizeIn  = input.string("small", "Text Size", options = ["tiny", "small", "normal", "large"], group = g_lab)
textCol     = input.color(color.new(#000000, 10), "Text Color", group = g_lab)

g_col = "Colors"
c1    = input.color(#c8cbd0, "Q1", group = g_col, inline = "q")
c2    = input.color(#f0a8b0, "Q2", group = g_col, inline = "q")
c3    = input.color(#a6d6a6, "Q3", group = g_col, inline = "q")
c4    = input.color(#a3b8e8, "Q4", group = g_col, inline = "q")
c5    = input.color(#dcd3ea, "W5", group = g_col, inline = "q", tooltip = "A fifth week, in months whose Tuesdays run to the 29th or later.")
cNone = input.color(color.new(#e3e4e6, 100), "None", group = g_col, inline = "q", tooltip = "The cells that carry no quarter number: Friday, a dead week, and the weekend on symbols that trade it. Transparent by default, so only the border shows.")
h1    = input.color(#9ea4ae, "Q1+", group = g_col, inline = "h")
h2    = input.color(#e8808e, "Q2+", group = g_col, inline = "h")
h3    = input.color(#76c276, "Q3+", group = g_col, inline = "h")
h4    = input.color(#7898dd, "Q4+", group = g_col, inline = "h", tooltip = "The fill a cell takes when it lands on one of the marked sequences: the same colour as its quarter, only stronger. Set each of them to whatever reads best against the row above.")
hiOn  = input.bool(true, "Mark These Sequences", group = g_col, inline = "hs")
hotList = input.string("111,114,141,144,411,414,441,444,222,333", "", group = g_col, inline = "hs", tooltip = "The three link chains that take the marked colour, separated by commas. The default is every chain built only of 1s and 4s, plus the same number three times. A cell is marked off its chain whatever the row prints, so a session cell reading Asia is marked just the same when it sits on one.")

g_dsp = "Display"
borderW   = input.int(1, "Border Thickness", minval = 1, maxval = 10, group = g_dsp)
aheadN    = input.int(2, "Blocks Ahead", minval = 0, maxval = 4, group = g_dsp, tooltip = "How many blocks past the last bar each row draws, dashed, so you can read what is coming next off the same ladder: in the first 90 min of AM it draws the second and the third as well. Only the rows whose cells are a fixed length are drawn ahead, the week and everything below it. Set it to 0 to draw nothing ahead.")
cellsKept = input.int(100, "Cells Kept Per Row", minval = 4, maxval = 500, group = g_dsp, tooltip = "How much history each row keeps. TradingView allows 500 boxes in total, so a smaller number here leaves room for the slower rows.")


// ──────────────────────────── border colour ───────────────────────────────────
isDarkBg() =>
    bg   = chart.bg_color
    luma = (0.299 * color.r(bg) + 0.587 * color.g(bg) + 0.114 * color.b(bg)) / 255
    luma < 0.5

borderCol = isDarkBg() ? #787b86 : color.new(color.black, 10)


// ─────────────────────── which rows are drawn, and where ──────────────────────
tfs     = timeframe.in_seconds()
isDay1  = timeframe.isdaily and timeframe.multiplier == 1

en1 = showCyc4   and tfs >= 21600 and tfs <= 10368000   // cell = 1 year
en2 = showQtYear and tfs >= 21600 and tfs <= 10368000   // cell = 1 quarter
en3 = showSplit  and isDay1                             // cell = a quarter of a quarter
en4 = showWeekly and tfs >= 1800  and tfs <= 43200      // cell = 1 week, off on 1D and up
en5 = showDaily  and tfs >= 300   and tfs <= 43200      // cell = 1 trading day, down to 5m
en6 = show6H     and tfs >= 60    and tfs <= 21600      // cell = 1 session
en7 = show90     and tfs <= 2700                        // cell = 90 min
en8 = show225    and tfs <= 540                         // cell = 22.5 min, down to 9m
en9 = show5625   and tfs <  300                         // cell = 5.625 min, below 5m only

// each row owns one unit of vertical space; the slowest cycle sits at the bottom
y1 = 1
y2 = y1 + (en1 ? 1 : 0)
y3 = y2 + (en2 ? 1 : 0)
y4 = y3 + (en3 ? 1 : 0)
y5 = y4 + (en4 ? 1 : 0)
y6 = y5 + (en5 ? 1 : 0)
y7 = y6 + (en6 ? 1 : 0)
y8 = y7 + (en7 ? 1 : 0)
y9 = y8 + (en8 ? 1 : 0)


// ─────────────────────────── intraday: 18:00 onwards ──────────────────────────
MS_DAY  = 86400000
MS_6H   = 21600000
MS_90   = 5400000
MS_225  = 1350000
MS_5625 = 337500

// the shared clock: real time pushed by the shift, read in gridTZ
adj = time + shiftMs

// The time of day on the WALL CLOCK, down to the millisecond, and from it the
// milliseconds since the 18:00 open of the current trading day.
//
// It used to be counted as the time elapsed since local midnight, and that is
// the hour the script was out by on the changeover: the Sunday the clocks go
// back is 25 hours long, so 18:00 on the wall is 19 hours after midnight and
// the whole ladder slid an hour. Worse, the trading day that opened that
// evening then came out one calendar day short - Sunday rather than Monday -
// so it lost its day number altogether and took the week back to the 4th of
// the month before, which is what printed "Asia 4-F-1" instead of "Asia 1-1-1".
//
// Read off the clock face there is no such thing: the day opens at 18:00 and
// London at 00:00 New York on every day of the year. The milliseconds inside
// the second are taken from the raw timestamp, since no zone is offset by a
// fraction of a minute, and they are what keeps a 5.625 min boundary - which
// falls on a half second - in the right cell on a seconds chart.
tod = (hour(adj, gridTZ) * 3600 + minute(adj, gridTZ) * 60 + second(adj, gridTZ)) * 1000 + adj % 1000
md  = (tod - 18 * 3600000 + MS_DAY) % MS_DAY

// offset inside each cell -> exact cell boundaries, free of rounding
off6    = md % MS_6H
off90   = md % MS_90
off225  = md % MS_225
off5625 = md % MS_5625

iSess  = int(math.floor(md     / MS_6H))   + 1   // 1 Asia, 2 London, 3 AM, 4 PM
i90    = int(math.floor(off6   / MS_90))   + 1
i225   = int(math.floor(off90  / MS_225))  + 1
i5625  = int(math.floor(off225 / MS_5625)) + 1

dayStart = time - md


// ──────────────────────── day, week and month ─────────────────────────────────
// from 18:00 the trading day belongs to tomorrow, and +6h lands it on midnight
tday   = adj + MS_6H
dw     = dayofweek(tday, gridTZ)         // 1 = Sunday .. 7 = Saturday
rawDay = dw == 1 ? 7 : dw - 1            // Mon 1 .. Fri 5, Sat 6, Sun 7
iDay   = rawDay <= 4 ? rawDay : na       // only Mon..Thu are quarters of the week

weekStart = dayStart - (rawDay - 1) * MS_DAY

// EVERY QUARTER BOUNDARY IS A MONDAY, and the bar that opens a week can come
// out dated to the Sunday: a symbol whose week opens before 18:00 New York -
// the FX week opens at 17:00 - gives that bar a time of day earlier than the
// 18:00 open, so the trading day it lands on is the Sunday rather than the
// Monday. Compared against a Monday boundary that threw the whole week into
// the quarter before and started the next quarter a WEEK LATE. So a date that
// lands on the weekend is pushed on to its Monday before it is compared with a
// boundary. On a daily chart every date is already a weekday and this is 0.
wkSnap = rawDay == 6 ? 2 : rawDay == 7 ? 1 : 0

// midday of the trading date, so adding whole days cannot slip across a DST edge
tdayMid = timestamp(gridTZ, year(tday, gridTZ), month(tday, gridTZ), dayofmonth(tday, gridTZ), 12, 0, 0)

// a week belongs to the month holding its Tuesday, and is dead when its Tuesday
// and Thursday fall in different months
tueTs = tdayMid + (2 - rawDay) * MS_DAY
thuTs = tdayMid + (4 - rawDay) * MS_DAY
dead  = year(tueTs, gridTZ) * 100 + month(tueTs, gridTZ) != year(thuTs, gridTZ) * 100 + month(thuTs, gridTZ)
iWeek = dead ? na : int(math.floor((dayofmonth(tueTs, gridTZ) - 1) / 7)) + 1


// ───────── the QT year, its quarters, and the quarters of the quarter ───────
//
// RULE 1 - THE YEAR AND ITS QUARTERS, which is what the weekly chart measures.
// The year opens on the MONDAY OF THE WEEK HOLDING THE FIRST TUESDAY OF
// JANUARY, and every week belongs to the year and the quarter of its own
// TUESDAY. The year is then four quarters of exactly THIRTEEN WEEKS, Q1 being
// January, except that in a 53 week year the extra week goes to Q4, which runs
// fourteen. Every quarter opens on a Monday and closes on the Sunday before
// the next one opens. 2026 is 5 January, 6 April, 6 July and 5 October,
// closing on 3 January 2027.
//
// RULE 2 - THE QUARTER OF THE QUARTER, which is what the daily chart measures.
// The quarter's TRADING DAYS are divided in four: a cell opens after a
// quarter, a half and three quarters of them, rounded to the nearest whole day
// with a half rounding up. Trading days are the weekdays less the exchange
// holidays below, and GOOD FRIDAY IS A TRADING DAY - the futures trade it - so
// it is not one of them. That is the one place this parts company with the
// equity calendar. Each cell runs to the day before the next, and the fourth
// to the end of its weekly quarter. Q1 2026 holds 63 trading days and reads
// 5 January, 28 January, 20 February, 13 March.

// Monday 0 .. Sunday 6
dowMon(int t) =>
    int d = dayofweek(t, gridTZ)
    d == 1 ? 6 : d - 2

// how many Mon-Fri days sit in the n days that open on weekday w0. Closed
// form, so it costs the same whether the span is a day or a year
wdCount(int w0, int n) =>
    int c = int(math.floor(n / 7)) * 5
    int r = n % 7
    for i = 0 to 5
        if i < r and (w0 + i) % 7 < 5
            c := c + 1
    c

// and back: how many days on from a weekday to weekday number k, w being that
// first day's own weekday
wdOffset(int w, int k) =>
    int(math.floor((w + k) / 5)) * 7 + (w + k) % 5 - w

// THE MONDAY THAT OPENS THE YEAR, in days from 1 January. It can come out
// NEGATIVE: when 1 January is itself a Tuesday the year opened the evening of
// 31 December, the year before.
yrOpen(int janTs) =>
    (8 - dowMon(janTs)) % 7 - 1

yrStart(int y) =>
    int j = timestamp(gridTZ, y, 1, 1, 12, 0, 0)
    j + yrOpen(j) * MS_DAY

// WHICH WEEK THIS BAR BELONGS TO, and from it the year and the quarter: the
// week's own TUESDAY. The bar's date is snapped to its Monday first, because
// the bar that opens a week comes out dated to the Sunday on a symbol whose
// week opens before 18:00 New York - the FX week opens at 17:00 - and its
// Tuesday would then be the week before's.
snapTs = tdayMid + wkSnap * MS_DAY
sDay   = wkSnap > 0 ? 1 : rawDay
tueQ   = snapTs + (2 - sDay) * MS_DAY

// the QT year this week sits in: the calendar year of its Tuesday, moved one
// either way when the year's own Monday falls on the other side of it
int qtYr = year(tueQ, gridTZ)
if tueQ < yrStart(qtYr)
    qtYr := qtYr - 1
else if tueQ >= yrStart(qtYr + 1)
    qtYr := qtYr + 1

yStartTs = yrStart(qtYr)
yEndTs   = yrStart(qtYr + 1)             // where the next year opens
q2Ts     = yStartTs +  91 * MS_DAY       // thirteen weeks each
q3Ts     = yStartTs + 182 * MS_DAY
q4Ts     = yStartTs + 273 * MS_DAY

iQtr     = tueQ >= q4Ts ? 4 : tueQ >= q3Ts ? 3 : tueQ >= q2Ts ? 2 : 1
qStartTs = iQtr == 1 ? yStartTs : iQtr == 2 ? q2Ts : iQtr == 3 ? q3Ts : q4Ts
qEndTs   = iQtr == 1 ? q2Ts : iQtr == 2 ? q3Ts : iQtr == 3 ? q4Ts : yEndTs

// the nth given weekday of a month, and the last one on or before a day of it
nthDow(int y, int mo, int wd, int n) =>
    int f = timestamp(gridTZ, y, mo, 1, 12, 0, 0)
    f + (((wd - dowMon(f) + 7) % 7) + (n - 1) * 7) * MS_DAY

lastDow(int y, int mo, int dom, int wd) =>
    int l = timestamp(gridTZ, y, mo, dom, 12, 0, 0)
    l - ((dowMon(l) - wd + 7) % 7) * MS_DAY

// a holiday on a Saturday is kept the Friday before and one on a Sunday the
// Monday after. New Year's Day is the exception: it does not move back to the
// Friday, so a Saturday one falls outside the week and costs no trading day.
wkndShift(int t, bool noFri) =>
    int w = dowMon(t)
    w == 5 ? (noFri ? t : t - MS_DAY) : w == 6 ? t + MS_DAY : t

// THE NINE DAYS THAT ARE NOT TRADING DAYS. Good Friday is NOT one of them.
holiOf(int y, int n) =>
    switch n
        1 => wkndShift(timestamp(gridTZ, y,  1,  1, 12, 0, 0), true)    // New Year
        2 => nthDow(y,  1, 0, 3)                                        // MLK, 3rd Monday January
        3 => nthDow(y,  2, 0, 3)                                        // Presidents, 3rd Monday February
        4 => lastDow(y,  5, 31, 0)                                      // Memorial, last Monday May
        5 => wkndShift(timestamp(gridTZ, y,  6, 19, 12, 0, 0), false)   // Juneteenth
        6 => wkndShift(timestamp(gridTZ, y,  7,  4, 12, 0, 0), false)   // Independence
        7 => nthDow(y,  9, 0, 1)                                        // Labor, 1st Monday September
        8 => nthDow(y, 11, 3, 4)                                        // Thanksgiving, 4th Thursday November
        => wkndShift(timestamp(gridTZ, y, 12, 25, 12, 0, 0), false)     // Christmas

// Worked out once per quarter and kept, because the count below walks the list
// several times a bar and each of these costs a timestamp. A quarter is at most
// fourteen weeks, so its own year and the one after it hold every holiday that
// can land inside it.
var int[] holTs = array.new_int()
var int   holY  = na
if na(holY) or holY != qtYr
    array.clear(holTs)
    for yk = 0 to 1
        for k = 1 to 9
            array.push(holTs, holiOf(qtYr + yk, k))
    holY := qtYr

// how many of them fall on a weekday in [fromTs, uptoTs)
holisIn(int fromTs, int uptoTs) =>
    int n = 0
    if array.size(holTs) > 0
        for i = 0 to array.size(holTs) - 1
            int h = array.get(holTs, i)
            if h >= fromTs and h < uptoTs and dowMon(h) < 5
                n := n + 1
    n

// trading days in [fromTs, uptoTs): the weekdays, less those holidays
tdCount(int fromTs, int uptoTs) =>
    wdCount(dowMon(fromTs), int(math.round((uptoTs - fromTs) / MS_DAY))) - holisIn(fromTs, uptoTs)

// THE SPLIT: the quarter's trading days in four, each cut rounded to the
// nearest whole day with a half rounding up
tdQ  = tdCount(qStartTs, qEndTs)
cut1 = int(math.round(tdQ / 4))
cut2 = int(math.round(tdQ / 2))
cut3 = int(math.round(tdQ * 3 / 4))

// how many of the quarter's trading days are already behind this bar
kIn = tdCount(qStartTs, snapTs)
iSp = kIn < cut1 ? 1 : kIn < cut2 ? 2 : kIn < cut3 ? 3 : 4

// the split row is drawn on the daily chart alone, so off it the level carries
// no number and contributes no link to a chain
iSplit = en3 ? iSp : na

// and back: the day trading day number k opens on. The weekday count is the
// first guess, and every holiday up to AND INCLUDING the candidate pushes the
// answer one weekday further on, which settles in a round or two. Counting the
// candidate in is what puts the first cell on the right day when the quarter
// opens on a holiday: 1 January 2024 was a Monday, so Q1 2024 opens on the 2nd.
tdDayOf(int k) =>
    int w0  = dowMon(qStartTs)
    int off = wdOffset(w0, k)
    for i = 0 to 5
        off := wdOffset(w0, k + holisIn(qStartTs, qStartTs + (off + 1) * MS_DAY))
    qStartTs + off * MS_DAY

sEndTs = iSp == 4 ? qEndTs : tdDayOf(iSp == 1 ? cut1 : iSp == 2 ? cut2 : cut3)

// the four year cycle runs 2023 2024 2025 2026: 2023 opens it, 2026 closes it.
// 2023 is 3 mod 4, so (year + 1) mod 4 numbers it without ever going negative
iCyc4 = int((qtYr + 1) % 4) + 1

// A bar's time is drawn at the middle of its candle, so half a bar back puts an
// edge in the gap between two candles rather than through the middle of one.
halfBar = tfs * 500

// WHERE A CELL STARTS: half a bar before the first bar that belongs to it, and
// nowhere else. The calendar start of the cell is no good here, because it is
// not always a trading day: 1 January 2024 is a Monday and a holiday, so the
// quarter's first candle is the 2nd and a left edge built off the 1st floats
// inside a data gap. A box edge inside a gap is placed by the average bar
// spacing, which drops it among the candles of the quarter before. Measured off
// the opening bar it cannot be more than half a candle from a real one.
slowLeft = time - halfBar

// WHERE IT ENDS, while it is the cell still running: its own calendar end, so
// the row reaches to the right like the one below it and the quarter's own
// length can be read off it. The moment the next cell opens, that edge is
// replaced by the next cell's left edge, in `cell` below, so a finished cell
// ends exactly where its successor begins, on a real bar, with no hole in
// between and nothing of the next quarter inside it.
dayGap(int t) =>
    int(math.round((t - tdayMid) / MS_DAY))

cycRight   = dayStart + dayGap(yEndTs) * MS_DAY - halfBar
qtrRight   = dayStart + dayGap(qEndTs) * MS_DAY - halfBar
splitRight = dayStart + dayGap(sEndTs) * MS_DAY - halfBar

// a cell is new when its own number changes, not when its left edge moves, so
// the edges above are read once, on the bar that opens it
cycId   = qtYr
qtrId   = qtYr * 10 + iQtr
splitId = qtYr * 100 + iQtr * 10 + iSp

// how far back the slow rows keep their cells: inside the current four year
// cycle from the weekly chart up, where the whole cycle is the point, and inside
// the current year below it, where older years are only clutter
cycFirst = yrStart(qtYr - (iCyc4 - 1))
// two days of slack, because a cell opens on the evening before its first date
slowTrim = (tfs >= 604800 ? cycFirst : yStartTs) - 2 * MS_DAY


// ──────────────────────────── label construction ──────────────────────────────
var int[] idx = array.new_int(9, na)
array.set(idx, 0, iCyc4)
array.set(idx, 1, iQtr)
array.set(idx, 2, iSplit)
array.set(idx, 3, iWeek)
array.set(idx, 4, iDay)
array.set(idx, 5, iSess)
array.set(idx, 6, i90)
array.set(idx, 7, i225)
array.set(idx, 8, i5625)

// The chain of quarters ending at level `lvl`, highest level first.
//
// A level that carries no number but is on the chart all the same, Friday and a
// dead week, holds its place with gapMark, so the session row reads week - day -
// session on a Friday just as it does on a Monday. Were the link dropped, the
// chain would pull the level above it in to stay three long and the same row
// would silently read quarter - week - session instead.
//
// The split level is different: off the daily chart it is not on the chart at
// all, so it is passed over without taking a place.
chainOf(int lvl, int depth, string sep) =>
    string out      = ""
    int    n        = 0
    bool   topFirst = chainOrder == "Highest level first"
    if not na(array.get(idx, lvl - 1))
        for i = lvl to 1
            if n < depth
                int    v       = array.get(idx, i - 1)
                bool   onChart = i != 3 or en3
                string piece   = na(v) ? (onChart ? gapMark : "") : str.tostring(v)
                if piece != ""
                    // the loop walks from the cell upwards, so the highest level
                    // first order is built by putting each new link in front
                    out := topFirst ? piece + (out == "" ? "" : sep) + out : out + (out == "" ? "" : sep) + piece
                    n   := n + 1
    out

dayNameOf(int d) =>
    switch d
        1 => "Mon"
        2 => "Tue"
        3 => "Wed"
        4 => "Thu"
        5 => "Fri"
        6 => "Sat"
        => "Sun"

sessNameOf(int sv) =>
    switch sv
        1 => "Asia"
        2 => "Lon"
        3 => "AM"
        4 => "PM"
        => ""

// What a row shows when it is named rather than numbered. The numbers come in
// as arguments, so a block that has not happened yet can be named off its own.
nameOf(int lvl, int yrV, int qV, int spV, int wkV, int rawV, int sessV) =>
    switch lvl
        1 => str.tostring(yrV)
        2 => "Q" + str.tostring(qV)
        3 => na(spV) ? "" : "Q" + str.tostring(spV)
        4 => na(wkV) ? "" : "Week " + str.tostring(wkV)
        5 => dayNameOf(rawV)
        6 => sessNameOf(sessV)
        => ""

nameFor(int lvl) =>
    nameOf(lvl, qtYr, iQtr, iSplit, iWeek, rawDay, iSess)

// names reach down to this level, numbers take over below it
namedDepth = nameRows == "Off" ? 0 : nameRows == "Down to the day" ? 5 : 6
numbered(int lvl) =>
    lvl > namedDepth

cellTextOf(int lvl, string nm0) =>
    string nm  = showLabels and not numbered(lvl) ? nm0 : ""
    string num = not numbered(lvl) and not appendNums ? "" : showLabels ? chainOf(lvl, labelDepth, sepStr) : ""
    nm + (nm != "" and num != "" ? " " : "") + num

cellText(int lvl) =>
    cellTextOf(lvl, nameFor(lvl))

// is this cell's three link chain one of the marked ones
hotSeq(string s) =>
    str.length(s) == 3 and str.contains("," + str.replace_all(hotList, " ", "") + ",", "," + s + ",")

// the quarter's own colour, in its strong shade on a marked sequence, or the
// colourless fill for a level that carries no quarter number
fillFor(int lvl, int i) =>
    bool hot = hiOn and hotSeq(chainOf(lvl, 3, ""))
    na(i) ? cNone : i == 1 ? (hot ? h1 : c1) : i == 2 ? (hot ? h2 : c2) : i == 3 ? (hot ? h3 : c3) : i == 4 ? (hot ? h4 : c4) : c5


// The numbers a timestamp falls on, worked out from that timestamp alone, so
// the same arithmetic can be pointed at a block that has not happened yet. The
// quarter split is left out: it is a daily chart row and is not drawn ahead.
idxAt(int t) =>
    int adjT  = t + shiftMs
    int todT  = (hour(adjT, gridTZ) * 3600 + minute(adjT, gridTZ) * 60 + second(adjT, gridTZ)) * 1000 + adjT % 1000
    int mdT   = (todT - 18 * 3600000 + MS_DAY) % MS_DAY
    int tdT   = adjT + MS_6H
    int dwT   = dayofweek(tdT, gridTZ)
    int rawT  = dwT == 1 ? 7 : dwT - 1
    int midT  = timestamp(gridTZ, year(tdT, gridTZ), month(tdT, gridTZ), dayofmonth(tdT, gridTZ), 12, 0, 0)
    int tueT  = midT + (2 - rawT) * MS_DAY
    int thuT  = midT + (4 - rawT) * MS_DAY
    int wkT   = year(tueT, gridTZ) * 100 + month(tueT, gridTZ) != year(thuT, gridTZ) * 100 + month(thuT, gridTZ) ? na : int(math.floor((dayofmonth(tueT, gridTZ) - 1) / 7)) + 1
    [int((year(tdT, gridTZ) + 1) % 4) + 1, int(math.floor((month(tdT, gridTZ) - 1) / 3)) + 1, wkT, rawT <= 4 ? rawT : na, rawT, int(math.floor(mdT / MS_6H)) + 1, int(math.floor(mdT % MS_6H / MS_90)) + 1, int(math.floor(mdT % MS_90 / MS_225)) + 1, int(math.floor(mdT % MS_225 / MS_5625)) + 1]


// ───────────────────────────────── drawing ────────────────────────────────────
var box[] bx1 = array.new_box()
var box[] bx2 = array.new_box()
var box[] bx3 = array.new_box()
var box[] bx4 = array.new_box()
var box[] bx5 = array.new_box()
var box[] bx6 = array.new_box()
var box[] bx7 = array.new_box()
var box[] bx8 = array.new_box()
var box[] bx9 = array.new_box()

// one box per cell: created when a new cell opens, and the row keeps only the
// last `cellsKept` of them so the slower rows never lose their boxes to the
// 500 box budget.
//
// Opening a cell also CLOSES the one before it, at this cell's own left edge.
// A cell's own end is a computed time, and a computed time can land in a hole
// in the data - the New Year, a holiday, a weekend - where TradingView places
// it by the average bar spacing and it drifts on to a candle that belongs to
// the next cell. The left edge of the cell that follows is measured off a real
// bar, and handing it to the cell before makes the two share one coordinate:
// whatever the gap, the rows meet exactly and no row reaches into the next
// quarter, month or week.
cell(box[] store, bool fresh, int left, int right, int top, string txt, color bg, int keepFrom) =>
    if fresh or array.size(store) == 0
        if array.size(store) > 0
            box.set_right(array.get(store, array.size(store) - 1), left)
        array.push(store, box.new(left = left, top = top, right = right, bottom = top - 1, xloc = xloc.bar_time, bgcolor = bg, border_color = borderCol, border_width = borderW, border_style = line.style_solid, text = txt, text_color = textCol, text_size = textSizeIn))
        if array.size(store) > cellsKept
            box.delete(array.shift(store))
    // and drop whatever now sits before this row's own cycle
    while array.size(store) > 1 and box.get_left(array.get(store, 0)) < keepFrom
        box.delete(array.shift(store))

l6   = time - off6
l90  = time - off90
l225 = time - off225
l562 = time - off5625

if en1
    cell(bx1, cycId != cycId[1], slowLeft, cycRight, y1, cellText(1), fillFor(1, iCyc4), slowTrim)

if en2
    cell(bx2, qtrId != qtrId[1], slowLeft, qtrRight, y2, cellText(2), fillFor(2, iQtr), slowTrim)

if en3
    cell(bx3, splitId != splitId[1], slowLeft, splitRight, y3, cellText(3), fillFor(3, iSplit), slowTrim)

if en4
    cell(bx4, weekStart != weekStart[1], weekStart, weekStart + 5 * MS_DAY, y4, cellText(4), fillFor(4, iWeek), 0)

if en5
    cell(bx5, dayStart != dayStart[1], dayStart, dayStart + MS_DAY, y5, cellText(5), fillFor(5, iDay), 0)

if en6
    cell(bx6, l6 != l6[1], l6, l6 + MS_6H, y6, cellText(6), fillFor(6, iSess), 0)

if en7
    cell(bx7, l90 != l90[1], l90, l90 + MS_90, y7, cellText(7), fillFor(7, i90), 0)

if en8
    cell(bx8, l225 != l225[1], l225, l225 + MS_225, y8, cellText(8), fillFor(8, i225), 0)

if en9
    cell(bx9, l562 != l562[1], l562, l562 + MS_5625, y9, cellText(9), fillFor(9, i5625), 0)


// ───────────────────────────── the blocks ahead ───────────────────────────────
// Drawn dashed, off the last bar, and only for the rows whose cells are a fixed
// length: the week and everything below it. Each one is read off its own start,
// so a block that runs into the next day carries that day's numbers.
//
// This clobbers idx with the projected numbers. Nothing below reads it, and the
// bar's own numbers are written back at the top of the next bar.
var box[] fx = array.new_box()

aheadOne(int lvl, int beg, int len, int probe) =>
    [c, q, w, d, raw, sn, n90, n225, n562] = idxAt(beg + probe)
    array.set(idx, 0, c)
    array.set(idx, 1, iQtr)      // the quarter is the year's own, not the month's
    array.set(idx, 2, na)
    array.set(idx, 3, w)
    array.set(idx, 4, d)
    array.set(idx, 5, sn)
    array.set(idx, 6, n90)
    array.set(idx, 7, n225)
    array.set(idx, 8, n562)
    int top = lvl == 4 ? y4 : lvl == 5 ? y5 : lvl == 6 ? y6 : lvl == 7 ? y7 : lvl == 8 ? y8 : y9
    array.push(fx, box.new(left = beg, top = top, right = beg + len, bottom = top - 1, xloc = xloc.bar_time, bgcolor = fillFor(lvl, array.get(idx, lvl - 1)), border_color = borderCol, border_width = borderW, border_style = line.style_dashed, text = cellTextOf(lvl, nameOf(lvl, 0, iQtr, na, w, raw, sn)), text_color = textCol, text_size = textSizeIn))

if barstate.islast
    while array.size(fx) > 0
        box.delete(array.shift(fx))
    if aheadN > 0
        for k = 1 to aheadN
            if en4
                aheadOne(4, weekStart + k * 7 * MS_DAY, 5 * MS_DAY, 2 * MS_DAY)
            if en5
                aheadOne(5, dayStart + k * MS_DAY, MS_DAY, MS_DAY / 2)
            if en6
                aheadOne(6, l6 + k * MS_6H, MS_6H, MS_6H / 2)
            if en7
                aheadOne(7, l90 + k * MS_90, MS_90, MS_90 / 2)
            if en8
                aheadOne(8, l225 + k * MS_225, MS_225, MS_225 / 2)
            if en9
                aheadOne(9, l562 + k * MS_5625, MS_5625, MS_5625 / 2)

// the rows are drawn entirely with boxes; this hidden plot only satisfies Pine's
// "at least one output" rule and is excluded from the pane and the scale
plot(0, "", display = display.none, editable = false)


// ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
//  QUARTER DIVIDERS
//
//  A vertical line on the candles wherever the chart's own container turns over:
//  the 90 min on a 5 minute chart, the 22.5 min on a 1 minute one, which is the
//  sub cycle each of those charts is read against. They are the two charts this
//  is wanted on, so no other timeframe draws one.
//
//  The line is placed half a bar before the opening candle of the new block,
//  like every cell edge in the pane below, so it falls in the gap between two
//  candles rather than through the middle of one, and it stands on exactly the
//  same instant as the row boundary underneath it.
//
//  Each line is also numbered with the quarter it opens - Q1 to Q4, the same
//  number the row below gives that block - written on the line itself. Pine is
//  not told where the chart is scrolled to, so the height is taken from the
//  last hundred candles: the middle of their range, or their high or their low.
// ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════

g_vl = "Quarter Dividers"
vlOn      = input.bool(true, "Enable", group = g_vl, tooltip = "A vertical line at every turn of the block the chart is read against: each 90 min on the 5 minute chart, each 22.5 min on the 1 minute chart. Nothing is drawn on any other timeframe.")
vlCol     = input.color(color.new(#787b86, 35), "Colour", inline = "vl", group = g_vl)
vlStyleIn = input.string("Dotted", "Line style", options = ["Solid", "Dotted", "Dashed"], inline = "vl", group = g_vl)
vlWidth   = input.int(1, "Width", minval = 1, maxval = 5, inline = "vl", group = g_vl)
vlKeep    = input.int(50, "Lines Kept", minval = 2, maxval = 200, group = g_vl, tooltip = "How many of them stay on the chart. TradingView allows 500 lines in total and the Devil marks want their share, so this is where to give some back if they ever run out.")
vlNumOn   = input.bool(true, "Number each line", inline = "vn", group = g_vl)
vlNumCol  = input.color(#787b86, "", inline = "vn", group = g_vl)
vlNumPos  = input.string("Middle", "Height", options = ["Top", "Middle", "Bottom"], inline = "vn2", group = g_vl, tooltip = "Which quarter of its container the block opening on this line is: Q1 to Q4, the same number the row below gives it. Pine cannot see where the chart is scrolled to, so the height is read off the last hundred candles - the middle of their range, their high, or their low.")
vlNumSize = input.string("small", "Size", options = ["tiny", "small", "normal", "large"], inline = "vn2", group = g_vl)

vlStyle   = vlStyleIn == "Solid" ? line.style_solid : vlStyleIn == "Dotted" ? line.style_dotted : line.style_dashed
vlTxtSize = vlNumSize == "tiny" ? size.tiny : vlNumSize == "normal" ? size.normal : vlNumSize == "large" ? size.large : size.small

// the block this chart is read against, and nothing on any other timeframe
vlTf  = tfs == 300 or tfs == 60
vlAt  = tfs == 300 ? l90 : l225
vlNum = tfs == 300 ? i90 : i225

// where the number sits, off the last hundred candles
vlHi = nz(ta.highest(high, 100), high)
vlLo = nz(ta.lowest(low, 100), low)
vlY  = vlNumPos == "Top" ? vlHi : vlNumPos == "Bottom" ? vlLo : (vlHi + vlLo) / 2

var line[]  vls  = array.new<line>()
var label[] vlbs = array.new<label>()

if vlOn and vlTf and vlAt != vlAt[1]
    // two points on one instant, extended both ways: a line up the whole pane
    array.push(vls, line.new(vlAt - halfBar, close, vlAt - halfBar, close + syminfo.mintick, xloc = xloc.bar_time, extend = extend.both, color = vlCol, style = vlStyle, width = vlWidth, force_overlay = true))
    // the number rides on the line itself: no bubble, just the text
    array.push(vlbs, label.new(vlAt - halfBar, vlY, vlNumOn ? "Q" + str.tostring(vlNum) : "", xloc = xloc.bar_time, style = label.style_none, textcolor = vlNumCol, size = vlTxtSize, force_overlay = true))
    while array.size(vls) > vlKeep
        line.delete(array.shift(vls))
        label.delete(array.shift(vlbs))


// ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
//  DEVIL MARKS
//
//  Any candle whose body runs to one end with no wick there, or a wick small
//  enough to ignore, leaves a short line at that edge. Direction does not
//  matter. A mark is only born once the candle has CLOSED, because a live
//  candle can still grow the wick that would disqualify it. It then stays only
//  while that area is untouched: once price trades through it the wick has been
//  filled in and the mark is gone, and that part is checked live, not on close.
//  Drawn on the candles with force_overlay, like the quarter dividers.
// ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════

g_dev = "Devil Marks"
devOn      = input.bool(true, "Enable", group = g_dev)
devWickTol = input.float(2.0, "Count as no wick up to this size", minval = 0, step = 0.25, group = g_dev, tooltip = "Measured in price, not ticks. A wick this size or smaller still counts as missing, so a one point tail on MNQ is still a Devil at the default of 2. Set it to 0 to demand a perfectly flat edge.")
devLen     = input.int(10, "Line length (bars)", minval = 1, maxval = 200, group = g_dev, tooltip = "How far right of its own candle the line runs. It is a fixed stub and does not follow the chart's right edge.")
devClear   = input.bool(true, "Remove once price trades through the level", group = g_dev, tooltip = "ON: the mark goes the moment price trades past it, because the missing wick has now been filled in. Checked on every tick, not only on close.\nOFF: every mark stays until the max pushes it out.")
devMax     = input.int(10, "Max marks kept", minval = 1, maxval = 200, group = g_dev, tooltip = "Once this many marks are live the oldest is dropped. Raise it for more history, lower it if the chart feels crowded.")
devStyleIn = input.string("Solid", "Line style", options = ["Solid", "Dotted", "Dashed"], inline = "ds", group = g_dev)
devWidth   = input.int(1, "Width", minval = 1, maxval = 5, inline = "ds", group = g_dev)
devLabelOn = input.bool(true, "Show label text", group = g_dev)

devLower    = input.bool(true, "Missing LOWER wick", inline = "dl", group = g_dev)
devColLow   = input.color(#26a69a, "Colour", inline = "dl", group = g_dev)
devTextLow  = input.string("Devil 👹", "Lower label text", group = g_dev)

devUpper    = input.bool(true, "Missing UPPER wick", inline = "du", group = g_dev)
devColHigh  = input.color(#ef5350, "Colour", inline = "du", group = g_dev)
devTextHigh = input.string("Devil 👹", "Upper label text", group = g_dev)

devStyle = devStyleIn == "Solid" ? line.style_solid : devStyleIn == "Dotted" ? line.style_dotted : line.style_dashed

var line[]  devLn  = array.new<line>()
var label[] devLb  = array.new<label>()
var float[] devPx  = array.new<float>()
var bool[]  devLow = array.new<bool>()

// Half a tick of slack on top of the chosen size, so float representation can
// never hide an edge that is flat to the tick.
devEps   = syminfo.mintick * 0.5
bodyLow  = math.min(open, close)
bodyHigh = math.max(open, close)
noLowerWick = (bodyLow - low)   <= devWickTol + devEps
noUpperWick = (high - bodyHigh) <= devWickTol + devEps

addDevil(float px, bool isLow, color col, string txt) =>
    array.push(devLn, line.new(bar_index, px, bar_index + devLen, px, color = col, style = devStyle, width = devWidth, force_overlay = true))
    array.push(devLb, label.new(bar_index + devLen, px, devLabelOn ? txt : "", color = #00000000, textcolor = col, size = size.small, style = label.style_label_left, force_overlay = true))
    array.push(devPx, px)
    array.push(devLow, isLow)

dropDevil(int i) =>
    line.delete(array.get(devLn, i))
    label.delete(array.get(devLb, i))
    array.remove(devLn, i)
    array.remove(devLb, i)
    array.remove(devPx, i)
    array.remove(devLow, i)

if devOn
    // born on close only: a live candle has not finished deciding its wicks
    if barstate.isconfirmed
        if devLower and noLowerWick
            addDevil(low, true, devColLow, devTextLow)
        if devUpper and noUpperWick
            addDevil(high, false, devColHigh, devTextHigh)
        while array.size(devLn) > devMax
            dropDevil(0)

    // walk backwards, so removing an entry cannot skip the next one
    if devClear and array.size(devLn) > 0
        for i = array.size(devLn) - 1 to 0
            float px = array.get(devPx, i)
            if array.get(devLow, i) ? low < px - devEps : high > px + devEps
                dropDevil(i)

alertcondition(devOn and devLower and noLowerWick and barstate.isconfirmed, title = "Devil - no lower wick", message = "Sav FX: candle closed with no lower wick")
alertcondition(devOn and devUpper and noUpperWick and barstate.isconfirmed, title = "Devil - no upper wick", message = "Sav FX: candle closed with no upper wick")
